+## September 7 continuation: selector and merged reduction closure
+
+The `226ac68e` CI checkpoint passed nineteen of twenty dedicated tests. Its
+remaining dedicated failure expected one parenthesization of a valid symbolic
+sign-bit expression. The broader regression also exposed real merged 59d
+widening-publication failures; those are separate from that text assertion.
+This continuation corrects the assertion without changing RTL and repairs the
+following generic ownership boundaries:
+
+- Publication and strict-observer snapshots precede the caller-controlled
+  publication selector. Both are checked for freshness after selector execution,
+  including an opt-out result, so a selector cannot mutate and then recapture
+  a signed graph or suppress stale-evidence rejection by returning false.
+- Exact native signed resizes are captured under cleanup as well as legacy
+  publication. The existing native assignment publisher owns their symbolic
+  geometry; the signed expression policy validates its operand evidence and
+  delegates only the original identity-owned resize. A clone or substituted
+  operand cannot acquire that permission. Other expression resizes continue
+  through the existing signed policy.
+- Retained zero initialization selects one exact native literal spelling from
+  the normalized literal kind and active printer mode. A signed native zero is
+  not confused with its unsigned spelling, and neither spelling is accepted
+  merely as a textual alternative. Existing identity and emitted-edge counts
+  remain mandatory before publishing the symbolic zero replication.
+- The native structural template parser treats the Verilog `signed` keyword
+  and native `$signed` calls as syntax, not signal references. Actual signal
+  identities, item ownership and overlap rejection are unchanged. This avoids
+  accidental ownership of every unrelated signed declaration.
+
+The compatibility suite now contains twenty-five tests, with no inherited test
+removed. The additional tests include true/false selector mutations, exact and
+foreign resize identities, stale operands preserving an existing output,
+signed/unsigned synchronous reset and hold at WIDTH overrides 1/5/8/12 under
+all three modes (including one-bit and hexadecimal native literal witnesses),
+and two independent signed reductions parsed at COUNT overrides 1/2/3.
+The twenty-five-test suite passes locally on Scala 2.12.18 and 2.13.12; the
+latter run includes the merged 59c implementation. Full widening, hardware
+qualification, exact-head CI, review and merge remain pending. Local source and
+compiler checks do not substitute for those gates.
+
+The current source ledger reverses the 60g changes before the existing 59c,
+59d and 59f restoration layers. Their historical contracts, independent RTL
+references and current canonical native audit remain intact. No 59c feature
+source is overwritten by the integration, and its whole source inventory and
+mutation checks remain required in the inherited closure.
